use std
into eml.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace eml). It reads RFC 5322 e-mail messages (.eml files, also with a leading mbox "From " line) with MIME: header unfolding, RFC 2047 encoded words, RFC 2231 parameters, multipart recursion, base64 and quoted-printable bodies, character sets, forwarded messages and attachments, which it keeps in module storage for the caller. Every module-level name starts with `em_` or `EM_`. All appends to the document model go through `dm::dm_add`, `dm::dm_set_meta` and `dm::dm_warn_add`; block kinds are `dm::DM_PARA`, `dm::DM_NOTE` and `dm::DM_FIELD`. Other modules it uses, all in the same folder: `html::ht_read_utf8(text const byte[:]) bool` (reads an HTML page that is already UTF-8 into the model, false when it has no tag), `html::ht_lower(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` (clears out and appends s lower-cased), `html::ht_find_ci(s const byte[:], pat const byte[:], from int) int` (case-insensitive search with a lower-case pattern, minus one when absent) and `textfmt::tx_text(data const byte[:]) bool` (reads plain text into the model, false when it looks binary).

```amni
import dm;
import html;
import textfmt;
```

Attachment storage: `struct EmAtt` has `nat int`, `nlen int` (the name in `em_apool`), `tat int`, `tlen int` (the media type in `em_apool`) and `dat int`, `dlen int` (the decoded data in `em_apool`). `var em_atts EmAtt[>..]` and `var em_apool byte[>..]` are module variables.

`em_att_count() int` is the number of attachments kept. `em_att_name(i int, out byte[>..]&)`, `em_att_type(i int, out byte[>..]&)` and `em_att_data(i int, out byte[>..]&)` clear out and append the name, the media type (lower case, like "image/png") or the decoded bytes of attachment i; they leave out empty when i is out of range.

`em_hexv(c byte) int` is the value of a hex digit (either case) or minus one.

`em_ws(c byte) bool` is true for space, tab, newline and carriage return.

`em_b64(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends the bytes that base64 text s decodes to: bytes outside the alphabet A-Z a-z 0-9 plus slash (white space, line breaks, anything else) are skipped, an equals sign ends the data, and a missing last padding is fine (leftover bits are dropped; two leftover characters give one byte, three give two).

`em_qp(s const byte[:], hdr bool, out byte[>..]&)` appends the quoted-printable decoding of s: an equals sign followed by two hex digits (either case) is that byte; an equals sign followed by a newline, or a carriage return and newline, or by spaces and tabs and then a newline, is a soft line break and produces nothing; an equals sign followed by anything else is kept as is; when hdr is true an underscore is a space; every other byte is copied.

`em_charset(cs const byte[:], data const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends data converted to UTF-8 for the character set named cs (compare ignoring case, ignoring a trailing "*" and language tag like "*en", and ignoring quotes and spaces): iso-8859-1, iso8859-1, latin1, l1, iso-8859-15, windows-1252 and cp1252 map every byte through `dm::dm_put_cp(out, dm::dm_cp1252(byte))`; macintosh, mac and x-mac-roman use `dm::dm_macroman` the same way; utf-16le and utf-16be use `dm::dm_utf16(data, big, out)` with big true for utf-16be; every other name (utf-8, us-ascii, utf-16 with a mark, unknown or empty) calls `dm::dm_to_utf8(data, out)`.

`em_words(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and appends s with RFC 2047 encoded words decoded. An encoded word is an equals sign and question mark, a charset (no question mark or white space), a question mark, the letter B or Q (either case), a question mark, text (no question mark or white space), a question mark and an equals sign. Decode its text with `em_b64` for B or `em_qp` with hdr true for Q, convert with `em_charset` and append the result. White space that lies only between two encoded words is dropped. Everything that is not an encoded word is appended as is, except that a run of it holding bytes of 128 or more goes through `dm::dm_to_utf8` first (so raw UTF-8 or Windows-1252 headers come out as UTF-8). Text that looks like an encoded word but is not well formed is copied unchanged.

`em_split(msg const byte[:], hend int&) int` finds where the header block of an entity ends: it looks for the first empty line (a newline directly after a newline, with an optional carriage return before either); hend is the index where the header block ends (before the line break that precedes the empty line) and the return value is the index where the body starts (after the empty line). When there is no empty line both are the length of msg.

`em_header(h const byte[:], name const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out and looks through the header block h for the first header called name (name is lower case; compare the header's name ignoring case). A header starts at the beginning of a line that does not begin with a space or tab and runs to the first colon; its value is everything after the colon with each folded continuation line (a line that begins with a space or tab) joined on by removing the line break and keeping its leading white space, and with leading and trailing white space trimmed. Returns true when found.

`em_param(value const byte[:], name const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` reads a parameter of a structured header value such as Content-Type or Content-Disposition (name is lower case) and clears out first. Skip to the first semicolon (the part before it is the type or disposition). Split the rest into parameters at semicolons that are outside double quotes. For each parameter trim white space, find the first equals sign, take the key before it (trimmed, lower-cased) and the value after it: when the value starts with a double quote it is the text up to the closing quote with every backslash removed (the byte after a backslash is kept), otherwise it is the text up to white space. A key equal to name gives `plain`. A key equal to name plus an asterisk (an RFC 2231 extended value) or to name plus an asterisk and digits, optionally followed by another asterisk (a continuation piece), contributes to `ext`, a byte buffer: pieces are appended in the order they appear; a piece whose key ends in an asterisk is percent-decoded (a percent sign and two hex digits make one byte) and when it is the single extended value or the piece numbered 0 its text up to the first apostrophe is the character set `extcs` and the text up to the second apostrophe (the language) is skipped before decoding; a piece whose key does not end in an asterisk is appended as it is. At the end: when `ext` is not empty append `em_charset(extcs, ext)` to out and return true; otherwise when a plain value was found append `em_words` of it and return true; otherwise return false.

`em_ctype(ct const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and appends the media type of a Content-Type value: the text before the first semicolon, trimmed and lower-cased.

`em_decode(body const byte[:], cte const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and appends the body decoded by the Content-Transfer-Encoding cte (compare trimmed and ignoring case): base64 uses `em_b64`, quoted-printable uses `em_qp` with hdr false, anything else (7bit, 8bit, binary or empty) copies the body.

`em_add_att(name const byte[:], ctype const byte[:], data const byte[:])` keeps an attachment: append the name, the type and the data to `em_apool` and push an `EmAtt` with their places; the name is "attachment" when it is empty. Then add a `dm::DM_NOTE` block (level 0) with the text "Attachment: ", the name, " (", the type, ", ", the number of data bytes and " bytes)".

`em_split_parts(body const byte[:], boundary const byte[:], starts int[>..]&, ends int[>..]&)` clears both arrays and finds the parts of a multipart body line by line. A delimiter line is a line that begins with two minus signs followed by the boundary and then either two more minus signs (the closing delimiter) or only spaces and tabs before the line end. A part starts at the line after a delimiter and ends before the line break that precedes the next delimiter (the break belongs to the delimiter, so the part excludes the last carriage return and newline); the text before the first delimiter and after the closing delimiter is ignored; a last part without a closing delimiter runs to the end of the body. Push each part's start and end index.

`em_retag(from int)` turns every block of `dm::dm_blocks` from index from onward into a note: set its kind to `dm::DM_NOTE` and its level to 0, and for blocks that were table rows (`dm::DM_ROW`) replace every tab byte in their text in `dm::dm_pool` with a vertical bar byte (124).

`em_text(utf8 const byte[:], html bool)` adds the text of a body part that is already UTF-8: when html is true call `html::ht_read_utf8` and stop when it returns true; otherwise (or when it returned false) call `textfmt::tx_text`, and when that returns false add the text as one `dm::DM_PARA` block.

`em_score(part const byte[:]) int` rates a part of a multipart/alternative body for reading: read its headers (use `em_split` and `em_header`), its media type with `em_ctype` (text/plain when there is no Content-Type) and whether it has a file name (`em_param` of the Content-Disposition value for "filename", or of the Content-Type value for "name") or a disposition that starts with "attachment". A part with a file name or an attachment disposition scores 0; otherwise text/plain scores 4, text/html 3, any multipart type 2, any other text type 1 and everything else 0.

`em_part(data const byte[:], depth int)` (a recursive function: `em_part` and `em_message` call each other, so both are declared recursive) reads one MIME entity (headers, empty line, body) and adds its content to the model. When depth is above 16 call `dm::dm_warn_add` with "email: MIME nesting too deep" and return. Split the headers (`em_split`), read the headers Content-Type (default "text/plain"), Content-Transfer-Encoding and Content-Disposition with `em_header`, get the media type with `em_ctype`, and the file name: `em_param` of the Content-Disposition for "filename", or when that fails `em_param` of the Content-Type for "name". Then: (1) a type that starts with "multipart/": take the boundary with `em_param` of the Content-Type for "boundary" (when there is none or no part is found treat the whole body as text, see rule 4); split with `em_split_parts`; for multipart/alternative read only the part with the highest `em_score` (the first one on a tie; when every score is 0 the last part) by calling `em_part` with depth plus one; for every other multipart type read all parts in order the same way; return. (2) The type "message/rfc822": add a `dm::DM_NOTE` block "Attached message:", remember the number of blocks now in `dm::dm_blocks`, decode the body with `em_decode` into a local buffer owned by this call (never a module buffer, because a nested message is read while the buffer is still in use and may itself hold forwarded messages), call `em_message` on that local buffer with depth plus one and nested true, and then `em_retag` from the remembered number; return. (3) A part that has a file name, or a disposition starting with "attachment", or a type that does not start with "text/": decode the body with `em_decode` and call `em_add_att(name, type, decoded)`; return. (4) Otherwise it is text: decode with `em_decode`, convert to UTF-8 with `em_charset` using `em_param` of the Content-Type for "charset" (empty when missing) and call `em_text` with html true when the type is text/html, else false.

`em_field(label const byte[:], key const byte[:], h const byte[:], nested bool)` looks for the header whose lower-case name is key in the header block h (`em_header`); when it exists and decodes (`em_words`) to a non-empty text, add a `dm::DM_FIELD` block (level 0) with the label, a colon, a space and the text, and when nested is false and key is one of from, to, cc, date or subject also call `dm::dm_set_meta(key, text)`.

`em_message(data const byte[:], depth int, nested bool)` reads a whole message: split the headers with `em_split`, call `em_field` for From, To, Cc, Date and Subject in that order (labels "From", "To", "Cc", "Date", "Subject", keys in lower case) with the header block, then call `em_part(data, depth)`.

`em_looks(msg const byte[:]) bool` is true when msg starts like an e-mail header block: the first line does not start with white space; every line up to the first empty line is either a header line (one or more bytes from 33 to 126 other than the colon, then a colon) or a continuation line (starts with a space or tab); and at least one header name, lower-cased, is one of from, to, cc, subject, date, message-id, mime-version, received, return-path, delivered-to, reply-to, content-type.

`em_read(data const byte[:]) bool` reads an e-mail. Clear `em_atts` and `em_apool`. When data starts with "From " followed by something other than a colon, drop that first line; when a later line starting with "From " follows an empty line, call `dm::dm_warn_add` with "mbox holds more than one message; only the first was read" and cut the text before that line. Return false when `em_looks` says the text is not an e-mail. Otherwise call `em_message(text, 0, false)`, then when `em_att_count` is above zero call `dm::dm_set_meta("attachments", count)` with the number as decimal text, and return true.
